    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The league of twelve cities, the Dodecapolis, was mostly an economic and religious alliance, a loose confederation like the Greek leagues; authority stayed with the individual cities and their leading families.</li><li>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> the twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> met once a year at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fanum-voltumnae">Fanum Voltumnae</a> at Volsinii, where they chose a leader, the zilath mechl rasnal, who was largely a ceremonial figure rather than a federal executive.</li><li>Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> held the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a> around 540 BCE, but Syracuse defeated them at Cumae in 474 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroyed Veii in 396 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> called themselves Rasna, 'the people'; the league's annual assembly chose a zilath mechl rasnal, a 'magistrate of the Etruscan people', and inscriptions speak of the Mechlum Rasnal, the 'community of the people'.</p></aside>
    </section>

